        "original_id": "d3848bb8-61a8-5fc8-b373-06086e73c007",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: North Hoyle Offshore Wind Farm, Liverpool Bay, Republic of Ireland\n- Projects: offshore wind power, offshore renewable power project\n- Vessels: SS Ellan Vannin\n- Water bodies: Irish Sea, Atlantic Ocean, Celtic Sea, Saint George's Channel\n\nFacts:\n- North Hoyle Offshore Wind Farm is Wales' first offshore wind farm.\n- North Hoyle Offshore Wind Farm is the United Kingdom's first major offshore renewable power project.\n- Liverpool Bay is a bay of the Irish Sea.\n- Liverpool Bay lies between northeast Wales, Cheshire, Lancashire, and Merseyside on the eastern side of the Irish Sea.\n- On 3 December 1909, SS Ellan Vannin was lost in a storm in Liverpool Bay after serving under the name \"Ellan Vannin\" for a further 26 years.\n- The Republic of Ireland is otherwise surrounded by the Atlantic Ocean, with the Celtic Sea to the south, Saint George's Channel to the south-east, and the Irish Sea to the east.",
